      "lineNbr": 314,
      "author": {
        "id": 1045980
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-12-20T03:30:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why are we doing this? d_fsdata is only written to when we allocate it, and then when it is removed. So long as the deletion is done in a RCU friendly way, I don\u0027t why we need this here, and in SDCARDFS_DI_R/SDCARDFS_DI_W",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 309,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 314,
        "endChar": 14
      },
      "revId": "9d6251bd6b7e4e69d43f9ace4fd3a3b6e1dcbeec",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "039a2339_a3a50c0d",
        "filename": "fs/hwsdcardfs/sdcardfs.h",
        "patchSetId": 23
      },
      "lineNbr": 314,
      "author": {
        "id": 1133869
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-12-20T03:59:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Because there existed a race condition.\nd_fsdata would be cleared after d_release but there could be still ongoing RCU dentry lookup still after \"get fs_data\" but before \"read_lock\", so we need the extra xchg lock.\n\nI don\u0027t know whether I describe the race condition above clearly. \n\nActually I don\u0027t like this approach too, so I have tried to use another approach \"ANDROID: sdcardfs: optimize relationship and locks (experimental)\" to take place.",
      "parentUuid": "a11001d9_985dce3b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 309,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 314,
        "endChar": 14
      },
      "revId": "9d6251bd6b7e4e69d43f9ace4fd3a3b6e1dcbeec",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9f2f60e8_7b7d2c25",
        "filename": "fs/hwsdcardfs/sdcardfs.h",
        "patchSetId": 23
      },
      "lineNbr": 325,
      "author": {
        "id": 1045980
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-12-20T03:30:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "These functions obscure the read/write locks, making it harder to see the paired read_lock and read_unlocks. These could be collapsed into one, raking the appropriate lock outside.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 325,
        "startChar": 28,
        "endLine": 325,
        "endChar": 41
      },
      "revId": "9d6251bd6b7e4e69d43f9ace4fd3a3b6e1dcbeec",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
